---
// src/pages/privacy.astro

import '../styles/global.css'
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Privacy — PayMeDash</title>
    <meta name="description" content="What PayMeDash stores, what it sends, and what it never collects." />
    <meta name="robots" content="index, follow" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
</head>
<body class="min-h-dvh bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-50 via-blue-50 to-indigo-100 dark:from-slate-900 dark:via-slate-900 dark:to-indigo-950 text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-100">
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 flex flex-col gap-8">

        <header class="flex flex-col gap-2">
            <a href="/" class="text-sm text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">&larr; PayMeDash</a>
            <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
                This page describes what the PayMeDash point-of-sale application stores and what it
                sends when a payment is taken. It describes this software, not any other site or
                service you may reach from it.
            </p>
        </header>

        <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">No accounts, no analytics</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                PayMeDash has no sign-up, no login, and no user accounts. It sets no advertising or
                analytics cookies and loads no third-party analytics, tracking, or advertising code.
                It does not build a profile of you and does not sell or share data with anyone for
                marketing purposes.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">What is stored, and where</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                Terminal settings are saved in your browser's <strong>local storage</strong> on the
                device itself, under the key <code class="font-mono text-xs">paymedash.config.v1</code>.
                These settings are the store name, the display currency, the tip percentages, and the
                DASH payout address the merchant has configured. They stay on the device. They are not
                uploaded to PayMeDash, and no server keeps a copy of them.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                Because the settings live in the browser, clearing site data removes them.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">What is sent when a payment is taken</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                To price a sale and produce payment instructions, the app sends the chosen payment
                asset and the amount to the PayMeDash API. The DASH payout address configured by the
                merchant is sent with that request so the funds can be delivered to the merchant. The
                API does not store this address; it holds no payout address of its own.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                The PayMeDash API forwards the swap request to <strong>1Click</strong>, the swap
                service operated at <code class="font-mono text-xs">1click.chaindefuser.com</code>, which
                arranges the conversion into DASH. That request necessarily includes the asset and
                amount you are paying, the DASH payout address, and a refund target. 1Click's own
                handling of that information is governed by its terms, not by this page.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                Refunds are issued to a NEAR Intents account held by the operator when you do not
                supply an address on your own chain. If you do supply one, refunds go there instead.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">What is never collected</h2>
            <ul class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
                <li>No name, email address, postal address, or phone number.</li>
                <li>No government identifiers, and no date of birth.</li>
                <li>No bank, card, or other payment-method details. Payments settle in cryptocurrency.</li>
                <li>No precise location data, and no address book or contacts.</li>
                <li>No passwords. There is nothing to sign in to.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Public blockchains</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                Payments are made on public blockchains. A blockchain records transactions permanently
                and openly, so the addresses and amounts involved in a payment are visible to anyone
                who looks, independently of PayMeDash. This is a property of the networks, not a
                choice PayMeDash makes, and it cannot be undone by deleting anything here.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="flex flex-col gap-3">
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The terminal you used</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
                The merchant operating the terminal you used is responsible for the sale itself,
                including what they do with the funds received and any records they keep. Direct
                questions about a purchase to that merchant.
            </p>
        </section>

        <footer class="pt-4 border-t border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
            <p>PayMeDash is open-source software. See the <a href="/terms" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Terms of Use</a>.</p>
        </footer>

    </main>
</body>
</html>